# =============================================================================
# CODEOWNERS -- EXAMPLE ONLY.
#
# Named .example on purpose. An active CODEOWNERS file naming real accounts or
# teams would publish an organisation's structure and the handles of the people
# in it. Copy it to .github/CODEOWNERS and substitute real owners when this
# repository has real owners.
#
# CODEOWNERS only has teeth when the branch protection rule for main requires
# review from code owners. Without that setting it is documentation.
# =============================================================================

# Default owner for anything not matched below.
*                                   @example-org/platform-engineering

# --- The parts where a mistake is expensive ---------------------------------

# Infrastructure. A change here can cost money continuously or open a network
# path, and neither is visible in a diff without knowing what to look for.
/infrastructure/                    @example-org/platform-engineering @example-org/cloud-security

# Anything that can deploy, publish, or hold a credential. A pull request that
# edits a workflow is a pull request that can change what runs with the
# repository's token.
/.github/workflows/                 @example-org/platform-engineering @example-org/cloud-security

# Scanner configuration. Adding a suppression is how a real finding gets
# silenced, so it is reviewed by the people who would have to answer for it.
/.checkov.yaml                      @example-org/cloud-security
/.gitleaks.toml                     @example-org/cloud-security

# The audit trail. Its append-only guarantees and hash chain are the evidence
# the platform produces about itself; weakening them is not a routine change.
/services/audit-service/            @example-org/platform-engineering @example-org/compliance

# Database migrations. Irreversible in production by default.
**/db/migration/                    @example-org/platform-engineering @example-org/data-engineering

# Security documentation and policy.
/SECURITY.md                        @example-org/cloud-security
/docs/security/                     @example-org/cloud-security
